-### Auxiliary grouped estimates
+### Filtered validation: data selection
 
-Figure 3, Extended Data Figs. 4–6 and Supplementary Fig. 2 use $N_{\rm b}=3,299$ non-overlapping 600-s means over 353.39 days and 54 segment constants. This reduction uses its own valid-interval selection, without the triangular filter or transient screening. Its solid-Earth template converts the IERS radial displacements into fractional frequency,
+Every segment is processed identically. The time axis is reconstructed from the first time stamp and the sample index. Samples more than 10 Hz (approximately $5\times10^{-14}$ in Yb/Sr) from the segment median are removed, the longest uninterrupted run is retained, and leading and trailing samples deviating from the median by more than 1% of the peak-to-peak range are trimmed. To remove smaller transient excursions, 300-s averages are compared with a running median over ±30 min, and averages deviating by more than six robust standard deviations are removed together with their neighbours. This step removes 0.4% of the samples, or 2.2 h (Supplementary Fig. 3). The criterion uses no tidal model, and adding the model to the data leaves the selection unchanged; because the subsequent filter is linear, injection tests use the selected data. Screening variants are given in Supplementary Methods SM2.
+
+### Filtered validation: tidal model
+
+The solid Earth contribution at each clock is
 
 $$
-y_{\rm solid}=-\frac{1+k_2-h_2}{h_2}\frac{g(u_{\rm Wuhan}-u_{\rm Shanghai})}{c^2},
+\delta W=\sum_m(1+k_{2m})V_{2m}+(1+k_3)V_3-g u_r,
 $$
 
-with $h_2=0.6078$, $k_2=0.3010$ and $g=9.794\ \mathrm{m/s^2}$, which differ from the IERS values of the primary model ($k_{22}=0.29801$) by 0.4% in $1+k_2-h_2$; its loading template uses the effective-potential convolution with 18 constituents. The eight named constituents form the lunar, near-12-h and diurnal groups, and the remaining terms form a fixed remainder $r_0$. The complete-template and grouped models are $y_i=c_{s(i)}+A_T(s_{L,i}+s_{S,i}+s_{D,i}+r_{0,i})+\epsilon_i$ and $y_i-r_{0,i}=c_{s(i)}+A_Ls_{L,i}+A_Ss_{S,i}+A_Ds_{D,i}+\epsilon_i$. The phase-relaxed model adds a quarter-cycle-shifted lunar template while keeping the M₂/N₂ relation.
+where $V_{2m}$ are the degree-2 tide-generating potentials of the Moon and Sun of order $m$, $V_3$ is the lunar degree-3 potential and $u_r$ is the radial displacement. Love numbers and displacements follow the IERS 2010 conventions ^16^ (Supplementary Methods SM1). The ocean-loading potential is computed by grid convolution,
 
-The weights are $W_{ii}=1/\sigma_i^2$ with $\sigma_i=\alpha_{p(i)}/\sqrt{\Delta t_i}$, where $\Delta t_i=n_i\delta t$ is the retained exposure of bin $i$ ($n_i$ one-second members, $\delta t=1$ s) and $\alpha_p$ is the white-frequency-noise coefficient of observing period $p$ from overlapping Allan deviations at 256–4,096 s: 9.70 and $5.70\times10^{-16}$ for September and October 2025, and $4.30$–$5.76\times10^{-16}$ for the six 2026 periods. Errors are the larger of the residual-scaled least-squares and UTC-day-deletion jackknife errors, each deletion refitting all coefficients. The contours in Fig. 3b show the scaled least-squares covariance at radii one and two.
+$$
+\delta W_{\rm ocean}(t)=\sum_{\rm ocean}\rho_w\zeta(\mathbf r',t)G_W(\psi)\,\mathrm dA,
+\qquad
+G_W(\psi)=\frac{ga}{M_E}\sum_n(1+k'_n-h'_n)P_n(\cos\psi),
+$$
 
-### Spectra and constituent separation
+with the FES2022 ocean elevation $\zeta$ on a $1/30^\circ$ grid ^17,18^, $\rho_w=1,025\ \mathrm{kg\,m^{-3}}$, Earth's radius $a$ and mass $M_E$, and angular distance $\psi$. The Green's function ^19^ uses PREM load Love numbers ^27,28^. After removal of the ocean mean, eight constituents (M₂, S₂, N₂, K₂ and the diurnal K₁, O₁, P₁, Q₁) are synthesized with astronomical arguments and nodal corrections. They explain 99.84% of the model variance; the remainder, with an r.m.s. of $0.18\times10^{-18}$, is held at its predicted value. The radial displacement from the same convolution agrees with the FES2022 load-tide grid to within 2.2% in amplitude and 1.2° in phase for seven of the eight constituents; for S₂ the grid derives from a different ocean-model release, and substituting it changes the full-template response by 0.001 (Supplementary Fig. 4; Supplementary Methods SM1). Extending the ocean mask near Shanghai by 0.3° or 0.6° changes the lunar response by −0.05 or −0.08, respectively, about half of its statistical uncertainty of 0.14.
 
