+The 1-s observations and model are filtered identically with a 1,200-s triangular window stepped by 600 s and fitted by least squares as $y(t)=c_i+A s(t)$, with an offset $c_i$ for segment $i$. For band analyses, the eight-constituent model is divided into semidiurnal (M₂, S₂, N₂, K₂) and diurnal (K₁, O₁, P₁, Q₁) bands, or into lunar (M₂, N₂), near-12-h (S₂, K₂) and diurnal groups with separate amplitudes. The primary grouped fit contains no empirical daily terms. Separate fits of the solid-Earth and loading templates assess their coupling (Supplementary Methods SM4).
 
-and plot the peak amplitude $A_y(f)=\sqrt{a_f^2+b_f^2}$; for the residual curve, the fixed unit-amplitude physical prediction is subtracted first. The equivalent height is $A_{h,\mathrm{cm}}=100c^2A_y/g_0$ with $g_0=9.80665\ \mathrm{m/s^2}$. The span $T_{\rm span}=353.3889$ d sets the nominal Rayleigh scale $1/T_{\rm span}=0.03275$ μHz. The scan spacing is $1/(12T_{\rm span})=0.002729$ μHz, with the astronomical frequencies added, and the peaks are local maxima within one Rayleigh scale of each prescribed frequency.
+For the full template, the semidiurnal band and the lunar group, uncertainties are calibrated by residual resampling. The fitted residuals are shifted circularly within each continuous run of the filtered record, added to the model with $A=1$ and refitted; the standard deviation over 200 realizations gives the error, retaining the within-run residual structure. For the diurnal and near-12-h groups, fits with empirical daily terms and fitted daily amplitudes, we quote the larger of the scaled least-squares standard error^52^ and the day-by-day jackknife error; in the resampled data these differ from the calibrated uncertainty by factors of 0.8–1.3. Group-bootstrap intervals draw the 29 operating groups with replacement in 1,000 refits; the segment bootstrap draws the 54 segments. In the phase test, each band has in-phase and quadrature templates, the latter shifting every constituent by 90°, with segment-bootstrap uncertainties. Quoted uncertainties are one standard deviation (Supplementary Methods SM3).
 
