+### Environmental, daily-disturbance and prediction checks
 
-$$
-u_{i,\mathrm{white}}^2=\alpha^2\sum_k\frac{H_{ik}^2}{\Delta t_k},
-\qquad
-\rho_{i,\mathrm{white}}=\frac{|\hat A_i|}{u_{i,\mathrm{white}}}.\tag{2}
-$$
+In the filtered validation analysis, daily sensitivity fits add $a_1\cos\omega t+b_1\sin\omega t+a_2\cos2\omega t+b_2\sin2\omega t$, where $\omega=2\pi/(1\ \mathrm{day})$ and $t$ is local time, with common or campaign-specific coefficients. Alternatives use, in each campaign, a free-amplitude 24-h term with the phase of the 24-h harmonic of hourly ERA5 2-m air temperature at the two sites ^25^, or the air-temperature anomalies at both sites relative to a 24-h running mean as two regressors.
 
-With the retained exposure $T=\sum_{k=1}^{N_{\rm b}}\Delta t_k$ of the $N_{\rm b}$ means, $s_{i,\mathrm{rms}}^2=\sum_k\Delta t_k\,s_i(t_k)^2/T$ for template $s_i$ per unit response and $\eta_i=\alpha/(s_{i,\mathrm{rms}}\sqrt{T}\,u_{i,\mathrm{white}})$,
+The response of the lunar estimate to a disturbance $B\cos(\omega t-\varphi)$ follows from the lunar row $h$ of the estimator $H$ (defined in the signal-to-noise calculation) for the specified fit. For a common phase, the maximum amplitude change is
 
 $$
-\rho_{i,\mathrm{white}}=
-\frac{|\hat A_i|s_{i,\mathrm{rms}}}{\alpha}\,\eta_i\sqrt{T}.\tag{3}
+B\sqrt{(h\cdot\cos\omega t)^2+(h\cdot\sin\omega t)^2};
 $$
 
-The sampling window, offsets, template coupling and weights enter $\eta_i$, and equation (3) gives the $a_i=1$, $b_i=1/2$ sensitivity at the measured amplitude (Supplementary Table 2). For M₂ and S₂ in Table 1, $\hat A=(\beta_c^2+\beta_s^2)^{1/2}$ from the equal-weight joint fit of M₂, S₂, N₂ and K₂ pairs with 54 segment offsets, and $u^2(\hat A)=JC_iJ^{\mathsf T}$ with $J=(\beta_c,\beta_s)/\hat A$; $C_\beta=H\Sigma H^{\mathsf T}$ with $\Sigma_{kk}=\alpha^2/\Delta t_k$ for the white-noise row, and the covariance of 43 UTC-day deletion fits for the measured row. The diurnal column uses the three-group fit. The loading column uses a fit of the complete solid-Earth and loading templates with two free coefficients, 54 segment offsets and equal weights, which gives $A_{\rm solid}=1.08\pm0.32$ and $A_{\rm loading}=0.49\pm0.54$ with UTC-day-deletion errors (Supplementary Methods SM6).
+for phases independent between campaigns, it is the sum of the corresponding campaign norms. The main-analysis bounds reported in Results use the three-group design, with the main sample times, lunar, near-12-h and diurnal templates, segment offsets and observing-period-noise weights defined in Methods. Figure 4 uses the corresponding operator for the equal-weight filtered validation fit. The following injection, environmental and held-out prediction checks use the filtered record.
+
+Injection tests add either the full tidal model ($A=1$) or the model without its lunar group ($A=0$) to circularly shifted residuals of the full-template fit, under four daily-disturbance scenarios: none; campaign-specific 24-h terms with air-temperature phases and fitted amplitudes; common 24-h and 12-h terms with fitted amplitudes and phases; and the fitted summer 24-h and 12-h terms. With 300 realizations per scenario and injected amplitude, the lunar-amplitude biases lie between −0.03 and 0.00, and for $A=1$ the fitted amplitude exceeds three standard errors in every realization (Extended Data Table 3).
+
+Campaign information shares project the lunar template onto the complement of the near-12-h and diurnal templates and segment offsets, and sum the squared projection within each campaign. Held-out prediction fits the amplitude to all but one observation period and applies it to the excluded period, refitting only that period's offsets (Supplementary Methods SM4).
+
+The round-trip beat of the fibre link, recorded in Shanghai near 10 MHz at 1-s resolution, is normalized by the optical carrier frequency and filtered like the clock ratio. It covers 474 h of the analysed data. Including it as an additional regressor, or excluding 300-s blocks in which it deviates by more than $5\times10^{-16}$, changes the lunar and whole-template responses by less than 0.005.
 
+The Sr vacuum-chamber temperature, the mean of 20 PT100 sensors sampled every 20 s, is available from 28 June to 7 July 2026 and overlaps 98 h of the filtered summer record. The blackbody sensitivity is $6.26\times10^{-17}\ \mathrm{K^{-1}}$ at 294.3 K. Because both clocks correct blackbody shifts in real time, a regression of the ratio on the chamber-temperature shift tests residual correction errors and has an expected coefficient of zero; it gives $0.92\pm0.74$ (Supplementary Methods SM4).
