 ## Introduction
 
-Optical clocks measure gravitational potential differences through the gravitational redshift. In the weak-field limit of general relativity, a potential difference $\Delta U$ produces a fractional frequency shift $\Delta\nu/\nu\simeq\Delta U/c^2$, where $c$ is the speed of light.^5^ Evaluated fractional systematic uncertainties now approach $10^{-19}$.^33^ Near Earth's surface, fractional shifts of $10^{-18}$, $10^{-19}$ and $10^{-20}$ correspond to equivalent heights of approximately 1 cm, 1 mm and 0.1 mm.^29,10^ Fibre networks and free-space frequency transfer connect distant clocks,^6,46,47,65^ and proposed quantum clock networks^44^ have reached an elementary demonstration with entangled clocks.^59^ Clock comparisons complement levelling and satellite gravity observations,^8,54^ have resolved the gravitational redshift within a millimetre-sized atomic sample,^10^ and support ultralight-dark-matter searches and gravitational-wave detection proposals.^38,62^
+Optical clocks connected by frequency-transfer links measure gravitational potential differences through the gravitational redshift. In the weak-field limit of general relativity, a potential difference $\Delta U$ produces a fractional frequency shift $\Delta\nu/\nu\simeq\Delta U/c^2$, where $c$ is the speed of light.^5^ Evaluated fractional systematic uncertainties now approach $10^{-19}$.^33^ Near Earth's surface, fractional shifts of $10^{-18}$, $10^{-19}$ and $10^{-20}$ correspond to equivalent heights of approximately 1 cm, 1 mm and 0.1 mm.^29,10^ The redshift has been resolved within a millimetre-sized atomic sample,^10^ while fibre networks and free-space frequency transfer extend comparisons to distant sites.^6,46,47,65^ Proposed quantum clock networks^44^ have also reached an elementary demonstration with entangled clocks.^59^
 
-Earth's gravitational potential also changes with volcanic activity,^60^ tsunamis^61^ and water storage.^55^ These contributions overlap in a clock record, so detecting a weak signal requires identifying the stronger variations and knowing how accurately each can be removed. Tides provide a predictable signal with which to develop this capability. Lunar and solar contributions must be distinguished from each other and from laboratory variations that follow the solar day, while observational gaps spread spectral power into neighbouring frequencies. Subtracting the tidal background therefore requires separating the tidal components and their sampling sidelobes and quantifying the precision of each component.
+Clock comparisons complement levelling and satellite gravity observations.^8,54^ Earth's gravitational potential also changes with volcanic activity,^60^ tsunamis^61^ and water storage.^55^ These contributions overlap in a clock record, so detecting a weak signal requires identifying the stronger variations and knowing how accurately each can be removed. Tides provide a predictable signal with which to develop this capability. Lunar and solar contributions must be distinguished from each other and from laboratory variations that follow the solar day, while observational gaps spread spectral power into neighbouring frequencies. Subtracting the tidal background therefore requires separating the tidal components and their sampling sidelobes and quantifying the precision of each component.
 
 Here we report measurements of tidal changes in the potential difference between a strontium clock in coastal Shanghai^21^ and an ytterbium clock in inland Wuhan,^22^ 692 km apart and connected by a 1,350-km fibre link.^24^ Over 545.9 hours of observations spanning 353 days, we measure the combined solid-Earth and ocean-loading tide and separately detect the principal lunar semidiurnal contribution (M₂) and the contribution at the principal solar semidiurnal frequency (S₂). The two contributions drift in relative phase and produce neighbouring spectral peaks, and each remains identifiable after the other is removed. We develop a signal-to-noise model that combines signal strength, noise and the actual sampling, and show that it accounts for most of the differences in precision between components.
